# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The important bit: never let a secret reach GitHub.
#
# Once a key is pushed to a repository it is considered leaked, even if
# you delete it afterwards — the history keeps it. Rotating the key is
# then the only real fix.
#
# .env.example is SAFE and should be committed: it lists the names of
# the settings with no values in them.
#
# This lives at the repository root so it covers every folder. It used to
# sit inside 02-the-project/, which meant nothing above that directory was
# protected at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# Downloaded code. Huge, and rebuilt by "npm install".
node_modules/

# Build output. Regenerated every time.
.next/
out/
dist/
build/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Compiled Python. The audit scripts and the site generator both produce
# these and neither is source.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Mac and Windows clutter
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor settings
.vscode/
.idea/

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Generated preview pages — the real pages are the ones without the
# preview- prefix. Path is anchored from the repository root.
02-the-project/website/preview-*.html

# The deploy package.
#
# `potatofarm-site.zip` is the ten pages and their assets, zipped for
# dragging onto Cloudflare Pages. It is built from
# 02-the-project/website/ and contains nothing that is not already in
# this repository, so committing it would store the whole site twice and
# guarantee the two copies drift.
#
# This project has already been here: its first commit was a repository
# containing exactly one file, POTATOFARM-MASTER.zip, with all 653 real
# files sealed inside it where nothing could read, diff or review them.
# A build artifact goes in a release, not in the tree.
#
# Rebuild it any time — the recipe is in website/DEPLOY.md.
potatofarm-site.zip
